{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1564315-would-the-tradition-mages-be-anti-vaxxers","id":1564315,"title":"Would the Tradition mages be anti-vaxxers?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2026-09-23T16:46:30-07:00","id":1564315,"title":"<img src=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/images/icons/icon5.png\" width=\"16\" height=\"16\" alt=\"\" /> Would the Tradition mages be anti-vaxxers?","content":"Would the Tradition mages use the waves of fake news to try to corrode the Technocracy paradigm?<br /> <br /> For example, would they defend the idea that the Earth is flat, that vaccines don't work, that evolution is not real and etc?<br /> <br /> In one hand, it would make sense (at least for the Traditions that don't rely on science), since in theory it would help drive the consensus against the Technocracy.<br /> <br /> In other hand... Yuck?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2518-erasculio","name":"Erasculio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2518_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"44"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-23T17:42:56-07:00","id":1564316,"title":"","content":"Some would.<br /> <br /> The Traditions' old guard in particular has a lot of folks who are anti-science and opposed to modernity in all forms. They're not as powerful as they've been in the past, but are still there.<br /> <br /> But they're not unified behind this, and crucially they wouldn't sacrifice their own Paradigms for aimless jabs against the Union. A Mage doesn't easily promotes a worldview they don't believe in, themselves.<br /> <br /> Most Tradition Mages, especially after the turn of the century with the Avatar Storm, believe in modern science and technology and that they are ok things. Not central to their beliefs, but ok things. They're not going to promote or accept this kind of complete bullshit.<br /> <br /> Yet some definitely believe, and being Mages they can actually find proof for their beliefs. So yes, there are anti-vaxxers in the Traditions and they're the violent kind who think this is a life or death matter.<br /> <br /> Worse, because the world of Mage is what it is, they may have a point: just because vaccines are conceptually real doesn't mean vaccination programs are all real. Plenty of entities in WoD are capable and willing to mess with vaccines, including the Technocratic Union. Some think it abhorrent as it would foment distrust if found out, but others wouldn't think twice before they put something in or even change the content to something else entirely. Reality Zones are even more complicated as they can change how the vaccines work within.<br /> <br /> Now, what kind of misinformation exactly will be spread depends. They are not equal, even if frequently supported by the same people. A lot more people doubt evolution than Earth being round, and vaccine deniers have a spectrum of rationales and positions that matter, <i>especially</i> in Mage. This game is about belief, the reason behind an opinion is as important or more than the position itself.<br /> <br /> Flat Earth in particular isn't a new phenomenon as we actually have known the Earth is round for a long time. Contrary to popular belief, the Church scholars of the Middle Ages were well acquainted with the fact the Earth is round and with several ways to demonstrate it, and also had to worry about idiots defending theories of anti-biblical conspiracies hiding the truth of the Earth being actually flat. We've known the form of the planet for millennia already, and even the exact circumference is known since the 3rd century BCE.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":4}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-24T01:05:27-07:00","id":1564319,"title":"","content":"I don't see why they would, unless their personal view of magic required people to believe in that kind of stuff.<br /> <br /> Some traditions might actually be super on board with it, like if you told the Order of Hermes that the Technocrats figured out a way to recreate their immortality potion in the form of pills.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29027-greathornedratkin","name":"GreatHornedRatkin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29027_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2026"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"413"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-24T02:50:06-07:00","id":1564321,"title":"","content":"The Trads include the Sons of Ether and the Virtual Adepts, definitely science guys. The books tell us that plenty of Hermetics and Verbena work as scientists. Both Celestial Chorus and Hermetics are frequently doctors as are Dreamspeakers. There are likely factions in each Trad that are anti-vaxxers. There are useless idiots in every group.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/857-astromancer","name":"Astromancer","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar857_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2195"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-24T03:40:11-07:00","id":1564322,"title":"","content":"One thing to remember is that most Tradition Paradigms don't really &quot;break&quot; if they believe in things like a round Earth or vaccines. The Earth is Divine and Alive, whether it's round or flat.<br /> <br /> The main pushback you get in the Traditions is from the Old Guard (which is to say, those 100+ years old) who oppose the Technocratic worldview on principle. Most Mages born within the last century carry a lot of cognitive baggage from their pre-Awakening days that never gets abandoned. Because why would they? Vaccines save mankind a vast amount of grief.<br /> <br /> Traditionalists likely debate this kind of thing all the time internally. Is it useful, or even moral, to dismantle the parts of the Technocratic paradigm that genuinely help people? And if Sleepers go around spreading pseudoscience and such, is it really okay to co-opt their movements to spread Traditionalist beliefs?<br /> <br /> Moreover, there's a massive slippery slope here, when we consider how often pseudoscience and science skepticism are intertwined with <i>Fascist movements</i>. The Flat Earth movement notably lost steam after a while...because a ton of their adherents moved to QAnon.<br /> <br /> (I'm of belief that any MtAs game set in Current Year has to reckon with resurgent Fascism in Real Life. And the way that, much like the 1930s/40s, a political philosophy that is both Anti-Rational, and built on reinforcing unjust power structures, is not a &quot;Technocracy vs Traditions&quot; divide. It's going to play out ACROSS sect lines. With members of both being forced to face others &quot;on their side&quot; that have embraced Fascism, for various reasons. Which, at the very least, creates interesting seeds for mixed Tradition/Technocracy cabals, or at least team-ups.)","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><div align=\"center\"><a href=\"http://archiveofourown.org/works/6956863/chapters/15862327\" target=\"_blank\">Magestuck: The Ascension</a></div>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/6156-bluecho","name":"Bluecho","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar6156_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"May 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3871"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-24T06:46:32-07:00","id":1564323,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1564315-would-the-tradition-mages-be-anti-vaxxers?p=1564322#post1564322\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Traditionalists likely debate this kind of thing all the time internally. Is it useful, or even moral, to dismantle the parts of the Technocratic paradigm that genuinely help people? And if Sleepers go around spreading pseudoscience and such, is it really okay to co-opt their movements to spread Traditionalist beliefs?<br /> <br /> Moreover, there's a massive slippery slope here, when we consider how often pseudoscience and science skepticism are intertwined with <i>Fascist movements</i>. The Flat Earth movement notably lost steam after a while...because a ton of their adherents moved to QAnon.</div> </div> </div> </div><br /> The Traditionalists were also helping people with their medical problems, but going about it in their own way.<br /> <br /> The Order of Reason and those affiliated with it did not approve of their methods, and so heavily persecuted them until the general public began to see them as frauds.<br /> <br /> Remember that in the grand scheme of Mage, reality itself is malleable. The world only works the way it does now because people believe that it does.<br /> <br /> If something were to fundamentally change that perception of reality, the universe would adapt to compensate.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/29027-greathornedratkin","name":"GreatHornedRatkin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar29027_1.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jan 2026"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"413"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-24T07:18:22-07:00","id":1564324,"title":"","content":"Traditions, as an institution, are more involved in reinforcing their Paradigms than trying to erode others. Not all Traditionalist believe the same thing and trying to erode A belief may end into conflict with other Traditionalists mages that have those beliefs. And, as said, many Traditionalists do believe in science<br /> <br /> So, the &quot;Traditions as an institution&quot; most likely does not endorse such campaigns - The party line it's &quot;to restore wonder&quot;, not &quot;to create more disbelief&quot;<br /> <br /> Of course,Traditionalists, as individuals, may be part of these campaigns. Either because their personal beliefs trully run contrary to modern science, because bad experiences with the Technocracy have them distrust modernity, or as part of a plot to gain personal power.<br /> <br /> I think the last one it's the most likely to get behind someting like anti-vaxx. No Belief that I know requires to believe vaccines are bad, and Technocracy using them as a vector to do something nasty woldn't be very &quot;mutual&quot; to the Progenitors that are trying to sell 'em as good.<br /> But a Mage (regardless of affiliation or belief) could prey on people who have their beliefs eroded, offering them an &quot;alternative&quot;. It could be used as a step on forming a Cult.<br /> <br /> It's a tactic that hurts people, and makes the world a worse place, but it's good for a cult leader ... I would say such a mage it's good material for an antagonist, and could very well justify their actions under the pretense of weakening Technocracy (if Tradition)","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-24T09:31:00-07:00","id":1564325,"title":"","content":"The short answer is yes, though it depends a great deal on what you mean. For instance if you mean anti-vaxxers of the conspiracy theory variety there are probably some among the technocracy too.<br /> <br /> What I mean is, though there is no reason to put it in your game ( and I wouldn\u2019t), it is actually very consistent with the Technocracy to use vaccines to accomplish secret goals without telling anyone. Just like it is consistent for them to be behind Project MKUltra. If someone is a vaccine conspiracy theorist because they themselves are actually spiking vaccines, does that count? I\u2019d say so.<br /> <br /> That said, as an ST you have to consider the players at your table. Just because it is consistent with the Technocracy doesn\u2019t mean it is good element for your game, so absolutely be ready to dump the idea if it will cause problems with your players.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Mage: The Ice-ension: An Epic Game of Reality on the Rink","edited":{"userid":758,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","user":"Ramnesis","date":".  Clarity 09-25-2026, 09:37 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/758-ramnesis","name":"Ramnesis","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar758_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2220"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-24T11:11:05-07:00","id":1564326,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Ramnesis</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1564315-would-the-tradition-mages-be-anti-vaxxers?p=1564325#post1564325\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">...it is actually very consistent with the Technocracy to use vaccines to accomplish secret goals without telling anyone.</div> </div> </div> </div>Tangential to your point, Project Pulse from the <i>Convention Book: </i><i>Progenitors</i> is more or less exactly that, but targeted at the NWO.<br /> <br /> If the NWO ever found out (which seems pretty likely), I could see many of them becoming very paranoid about any large scale medical programme.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><img itemprop=\"image\" class=\"bbcode-attachment\" src=\"http://theonyxpath.com/downloads/Backer%20Badge%20small.png\" border=\"0\" alt=\"\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/178-michael","name":"Michael","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar178_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3560"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-24T17:12:32-07:00","id":1564328,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Bluecho</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1564315-would-the-tradition-mages-be-anti-vaxxers?p=1564322#post1564322\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Traditionalists likely debate this kind of thing all the time internally. Is it useful, or even moral, to dismantle the parts of the Technocratic paradigm that genuinely help people? And if Sleepers go around spreading pseudoscience and such, is it really okay to co-opt their movements to spread Traditionalist beliefs?<br /> <br /> Moreover, there's a massive slippery slope here, when we consider how often pseudoscience and science skepticism are intertwined with <i>Fascist movements</i>. The Flat Earth movement notably lost steam after a while...because a ton of their adherents moved to QAnon.</div> </div> </div> </div>Yeah, this is my main issue with the idea. I would fully expect very old mages from the Order of Hermes to enjoy the pseudosciente movements just to spite the Technocracy; but to actually create and fuel those movements, for me it feels closer to what I would expect the Nephandi to do.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/2518-erasculio","name":"Erasculio","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar2518_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Mar 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"44"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-24T20:32:37-07:00","id":1564329,"title":"","content":"It mostly is, <i><b>IF</b></i> we assume real world conditions and reasonable people.<br /> <br /> But it's not the real world, and definitely not reasonable people. You have to consider a lot of things:<br /> <br /> <i><b>1. Mage is not about clear heroes against clear villains. All factions do horrible things.</b></i><br /> <br /> The Traditions already accept all kinds of abuse against Sleepers within their ranks, always have. Slavery, sacrifice, experiments of all kinds, almost nothing is off limits even if not approved. Those things are common enough for them to have names for those in a Magical context.<br /> <br /> <i><b>2. All WoD games are about monsters, Mage is no exception.</b></i><br /> <br /> Everything a Mage do is Magical, and Magic is a distortion of Reality. You can't prove a Mage wrong: their mere belief produce whatever evidence they need for anything they believe. Try to run that to its logical conclusion. No reality check is possible at theoretical level, because their fundamental nature is to force reality itself into compliance. A Mage can't, by definition, falsify their own beliefs. Ever.<br /> <br /> <i><b>3. From this, run bogus theories as someone who can invariably prove them for real.</b></i><br /> <br /> A Flat-Earth Sleeper has to contend with reality when they go to Antarctica and the sun doesn't set every day. A Flat-Earth Mage goes to a version of Antarctica where the sun sets every day.<br /> <br /> <i><b>4. Conspiracy theories are real.</b></i><br /> <br /> There's literally a New World Order manipulating governments and sending Men in Black to erase evidence of the paranormal, a Syndicate of market masters that control the flow of money and all banks, a secret society within the Health community is in fact hiding from you real treatments that go against the model of health they decided for you. Some do spike the water.<br /> <br /> <i><b>Unchecked beliefs turn ugly, and Mages are the result.</b></i><br /> <br /> I agree with you that the rise of Fascism in the game is probably a service to the Nefandi, but make no mistake: Mages are deep in it, and in everything that follows from ideology taken to its conclusion without reality checks. Fascism is the standard fare of most Crafts, even if it is also doing the bidding of their enemies. Mages can't be reasoned with easily if they don't put a lot of active effort themselves to limit their own hubris.<br /> <br /> So, yeah. To your original question the answer is that very few Mages would foment distrust in science and stupid theories just from spite. The problem is that there are other reasons for a Mage to do that, and they're way too strong in the setting.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2},{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":1},{"type":"\u2615","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-25T08:12:20-07:00","id":1564334,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>monteparnas</strong> </div> <div class=\"message\">A Mage can't, by definition, falsify their own beliefs. Ever</div> </div> </div> </div>A Mage can't easily be an hipocrite about their Belief (about everything else they can). Perhaps we could say that they're people driven to act in good faith (defined as, roughly, being honest with oneself) - they <i>can</i> be coward but not acting for what they think it's rigth it's a sure way to not be a very good/strong mage.<br /> <br /> BUT, importantly to this debate: A mage *can* lie (Like how J-P Sartre defined lies as fundamentaly of good faith, because the liar <i>knows</i> they're lying)<br /> <br /> They have a practical reason not to: Spreading their Belief it's convinient to both reduce the Paradox effect and gain adepts. Spreading <i>other </i>beliefs (or eroding other beliefs) doesn't have this beneffit, from an Ascencion War perspective it's just pissing on another's turf.<br /> <br /> Because of this, I actually think Traditions, <i>as an institution</i>, are the <i>less</i> likely to condone deliberate dissinformation campaigns. Traditions are too big and varied on their beliefs, it's more than likely that trying to erode someone's belief it's going to end affecting negatively some Traditionalist that has that belief. So, as an institution, they sort of have to be against sowing mistrust. The moto it's &quot;to spread wonder&quot;, not &quot;to spread disbelief&quot;<br /> <br /> At most one could say that Tradition mages have more reasons to <i>fall</i> into these schemes because the Techncoracy does exist.<br /> <br /> But <b>any </b>mage (regardless of affiliation) may chose to do dissinformation campaigns to gain a personal advantage, even if they don't Believe it. As long as it doesn't run contrary to their Belief, they can use any deliberate disinformation they want to gain an advatage.<br /> <br /> I.E: We know from real life that people that have their faith on institutions eroded tend to trust instead on some influencers or individual figure perceived ad going against the current. Mages, being notoriously strong-willed, could sow disbelief to then try and offer &quot;an alternative&quot;, as part of a scheme to build a Cult.<br /> <br /> It's a pretty nasty strategy, it hurts people and makes a <i>less</i> magical world (as this mistrust doesn't automatically translate to belief on &quot;positive&quot; outcomes) but one I can see mages using. And a Traditionalist using it could very well <i>excuse </i>their behavior as an attack against the Technocracy.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <i><b>3. From this, run bogus theories as someone who can invariably prove them for real.</b></i><br /> <br /> A Flat-Earth Sleeper has to contend with reality when they go to Antarctica and the sun doesn't set every day. A Flat-Earth Mage goes to a version of Antarctica where the sun sets every day. </div> </div> </div>It's important to note that there are Belief and beliefs. Perhaps we could say that not all beliefs are core to a Paradigm, stuff that isn't part of the Paradigm/Practice/Instruments can be easier to challenge.<br /> <br /> Like, of course you can't prove to a Christian Chorister that prayers don't heal. Their prayers do heal - a lot of people prayers heal, in fact. But if you were to sell a Chorister that low key believes on the Flat Earth a ticket to the Antartica, they won't just teleport to an Antartica where they're rigth. They're going to mundane Antartica like everyone else, and see what everyone sees.<br /> <br /> Oh, sure, they can potentially say &quot;NASA created a fake antartica to deceive me&quot; like mundane flat earther does - and if they're powerful enough, even make the effort to travel to the &quot;real&quot; one. But I think it's wrong to say most Mages work like this: Remember that as per M20 Mage beliefs systems are supposed to be solid and very encompassing. Mages don't normaly believe on the &quot;spaghetti monster&quot;, they believe on stuff that can pass the test of time and can be adapted to new discoveries - you know, like real Religions.<br /> <br /> And I would say Mages are less likely to fall on this kind of dissinformation than sleepers. Most people that falls on this kind of conspirations are people that <i>want to believe</i> - either they find that thier idea &quot;makes sense&quot; and want validation, because a person that they believe in at a personal level believes this, or just want to believe (or disbelieve) for, as said above, <i>very political </i>reasons.<br /> <br /> Mages don't require any validation. Mages don't believe things on bad faith because they're confy. They don't change their beliefs the way almost every flat-eather said they changed theirs: because somebody said something that &quot;just made sense&quot;. They're the kind of people that are actually going to fact check - on good faith <hr /><br /> I would even say Mages can even be <i>more</i> open about certain things than normal people<br /> <br /> Like how on the middle ages they were able to incorporate customs of many weird places because they were able to travel further than regular people, and meet more (and weirder) people (and that's why Traditions were considered to be more &quot;progressive&quot; about stuff like &quot;gender&quot; or &quot;religion&quot; than other Europeans of their time). Mages are more cappable of proving their beliefs than regular people, but are also much more likely to come across new and weird stuff that challenges them .<br /> <br /> Perhaps that's why there aren't many Flat-Earth mages, but there are many Hollow-Earth mages. The latter it's a place you can't travel to w/o magic, and one can argue that it's loaded to say it's &quot;another dimension&quot; just because you need to have special means of travel to access it (you can't just go to Mars either, and you don't say &quot;oh, it's on another dimension&quot; - or is it?). While the former it's denying what you can know on in many different and easy ways w/o even using magick - just by watching the sky, taking a plane, talking with regular people that went there, etc.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> I agree with you that the rise of Fascism in the game is probably a service to the Nefandi, but make no mistake: Mages are deep in it, and in everything that follows from ideology taken to its conclusion without reality checks. Fascism is the standard fare of most Crafts, even if it is also doing the bidding of their enemies. Mages can't be reasoned with easily if they don't put a lot of active effort themselves to limit their own hubris. </div> </div> </div>Agreed. The whole Ascencion War it's about it. We had &quot;the culture war&quot; before it was cool<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> So, yeah. To your original question the answer is that very few Mages would foment distrust in science and stupid theories just from spite. The problem is that there are other reasons for a Mage to do that, and they're way too strong in the setting. </div> </div> </div>Very much this.<br /> <br /> If people can gain power throug sowing dissinformation, so can Mages.<br /> \u200b\u200b","signature":"","edited":{"userid":1683,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","user":"Aleph","date":". 09-25-2026, 11:23 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1683-aleph","name":"Aleph","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1683_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"3056"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-25T13:28:40-07:00","id":1564341,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Aleph</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1564315-would-the-tradition-mages-be-anti-vaxxers?p=1564334#post1564334\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">A Mage can't easily be an hipocrite about their Belief (about everything else they can). Perhaps we could say that they're people driven to act in good faith (defined as, roughly, being honest with oneself) - they <i>can</i> be coward but not acting for what they think it's rigth it's a sure way to not be a very good/strong mage.<br /> \u200b</div> </div> </div> </div>You misunderstood me. Falsify is not about lie.<br /> <br /> Falsifying is a methodology, one of the cores of modern Scientific Method. Falsify is proposing an experiment to test a hypothesis with focus on its failure points. Instead of pointing to existing evidence of it working, you stress test it (or at least propose a future stress test)<br /> <br /> Gods are deemed outside scientific scrutiny because they are non falsifiable: whenever you predict a divine intervention will happen and have X effect, X not happening may have a lot of perfectly reasonable explanations, from the god being offended, knowing things you don't, to other gods intervening.<br /> <br /> Einstein's theory that light had mass was falsifiable, although it took years for an opportunity to test it to appear. If light has mass, it is subject to gravity, if it is subject to gravity, then any sufficient mass should work as a lens, and if all those are true, then it should be possible to watch a star, calculate its motion until it is behind a known massive body, and predict a point where it would be seen that is not its actual position, due to lensing. If the theory is wrong, the prediction will fail and will fail hard.<br /> <br /> That's the non-falsifiability problem in Mage: a Mage alters reality by nature to conform to the Paradigm, and so they <i>produce</i> the response in accordance to their Paradigm. Any failure is a mere fluke if execution or lack of skill, not the theory failing, and this is objectively true every time with them.<br /> <br /> No Mage can devise a stress test where their Paradigm is proven wrong, because any stress test exposes Reality to their power and change the outcome. No success or failure can be reliably attributed to the theory, only weakly so.<br /> <br /> And I agree that Mages have a tendency to be more open-minded. But not always. And not in a predictable manner. We see that clearly on many Crafts. The clash of diverse experiences with unshakable confidence in one's own view can and does produce truly weird outcomes.<br /> <br /> So, is any Mage a dumb follower of QAnon? It's unlikely. Are there Mages that include a lot of QAnon stuff in their worldview, even if the whole picture is more picturesque? Probably a bunch of them and with the type of confidence that foments action and justify wars.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>#NothingAboutUsWithoutUs<br /> #AutismPride<br /> She/her pronouns","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/5826-monteparnas","name":"monteparnas","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar5826_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Apr 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"6710"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2026-09-26T00:07:03-07:00","id":1564345,"title":"","content":"Sure why not, make the traditions Le based chud right. what could go wrong. <br /> Don't both sides it, don't make the technocrats some obese danger haired screaming woman. They still get to be some badass man in black, kicking down doors and taking names Just instead of fighting traditional hermetics they fight redneck hermetics. Endearing ones though, since they're player characters. Like Tucker and Dale vs evil. <br /> <br /> For that chronicle big pharma wouldn't be the technocrats they'd be pentex. Vaccinations gives you scaly tails and makes you eat people in addition to occasional heart attacks. Worse cases turn into some reptililian mutant and hide in the sewers.<br /> <br /> The people who still wear masks are more common and form small enclaves, but in this case they still wear masks to hide their mandibles. Like innsmouth people, or a genestealer cult. Pentex pretends to support them but really harvests them for parts.<br /> <br /> At the end of the chronicle the players have to team up with the technocrats to purge the enclave and take down the pentex facility.<br /> <br /> Possible would work better as a werewolf game.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4e","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/28924-tjalei","name":"Tjalei","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Sep 2025"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"81"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}